00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.080
So this is basically
what cloud consists of.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.410
Cloud is divided into regions,
which further get subdivided

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.940
into zones.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.890
Now, a region is a
geographic location

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.440
in a certain continent
where the Round-Trip

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.170
Time-- or the RTT--
from one VM to another

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.520
is typically under
1 millisecond.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.740
A region is typically divided
into three or four zones.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.330
And a zone is a
geographic location

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.810
within a region which
has its own fully

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.200
isolated and independent
failure domain.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.510
And so no two machines
that are in different zones

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.270
or in different regions
share the same fate

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.110
when it comes to failure.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.060
So they're definitely in
different data centers.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:46.830
In GCP at this time, we have
19 regions and 58 zones.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.850
So how does Google's network
infrastructure power this?

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.350
Now, Google's network
infrastructure

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.090
basically consists of three
main types of networks.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.408
The first is the
data center network.

00:01:56.408 --> 00:01:58.700
This is what connects all
the machines in a data center

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.330
together.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.840
The second is a
software-based private WAN

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.030
that we call before that
connects all the data

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.060
centers together.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.350
And the third is also
an SDN-based public WAN

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.720
for user-facing traffic
entering our network.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.930
So a machine basically gets
connected from the internet

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.630
via the public WAN, and gets
connected to other machines

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.350
and other data centers
over the private WAN.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.880
So when you send a packet from
your virtual machine running

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.160
in cloud, let's say,
in North America,

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.790
to a GCS bucket running
an Asia, for example,

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.580
then the packet doesn't
leave the network backbone.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.920
It basically doesn't
traverse over the internet,

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.707
it traverses over
Google's network.

00:02:42.707 --> 00:02:44.790
Now one of the things that
I would like to mention

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.240
here is that, in addition
to the peering routers

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.740
at the edge of our
network, we also

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.370
deploy network load balancers
and layer 7 reverse proxies.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.280
The layer 7 reverse
proxies basically

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.180
help us in terminating
the user's TCP or SSL

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.150
connection at a location
close to the user.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.510
Now this is really important
because, as you know,

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.010
establishing an HTTPS
connection requires

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.280
two network round-trips between
the client and the server.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.130
And so it's really
important to reduce

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.840
the RTT between the
client and the server.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.780
And we do this by bringing the
server closer to the client.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.210
And so we basically terminate
the end user's TCP or SSL

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.820
connection at a location that
is closest to the end user.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.270
So this is basically what
comprises the global Google

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.500
network.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.820
With 134 network edge locations,
presence in over 200 countries

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.090
and territories, and content
delivered through Google Cloud

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.420
CDN, you get access to
the same functionality

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.570
that also powers all
of Google's services,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.140
like Search, Maps,
Gmail, YouTube.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.092
Each of these are used by over
a billion users worldwide.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.020
This is a snapshot
of our footprint.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.610
In GCP, as I mentioned
before, we have 19 regions.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.710
We have also announced two new
regions that will be turning up

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.730
before the end of this year.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.390
So we are turning
up regions in Japan

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.753
and South Korea this year.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:16.170
Before the end of
next year, we'll

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.209
also be turning up
to more regions,

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:22.170
in Salt Lake City, USA,
and in Jakarta, Indonesia.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.800
As I mentioned before,
we have 134 PoPs,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:34.210
or Points of Presence,
and 96 CDN locations.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.090
Our data centers are connected
to each other through hundreds

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.610
of thousands of miles
of fiber optic cable.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:45.570
We also have 13 sub-sea
cable investments.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.000
We have 81
interconnect locations.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.550
So these are basically sites
where you can physically

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.270
peer with us directly in
order to use either dedicated

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.740
or partner interconnect.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.750
So why is all of this
important to you?

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.915
Why do you care that the
network is actually global?

00:05:02.915 --> 00:05:04.790
Now, if you were to
deploy your applications,

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:08.450
you would do so by deploying
the applications to multiple VMs

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:09.920
in a single zone.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.950
In order to protect yourself
against single-VM or

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.380
single-machine failures.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.870
In order to protect yourself
against single-zone failures,

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.340
you can replicate
your application

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.590
across multiple
zones in the region.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:24.320
So this basically gives
you added redundancy,

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.970
and so increases
the availability

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.160
of your application.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.660
In order to further
protect yourself

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.380
against regional outages--

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.700
so things like natural disasters
or other such rare events--

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.470
you can then replicate
your application

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.610
across multiple regions.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.340
And this is how you get
a global deployment.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.140
Now what is critical here is
that with global deployments,

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.340
you are assured that
all the traffic stays

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.120
on Google's network backbone and
does not leave Google's network

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.540
backbone.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:54.770
So you get access to all of your
compute and storage resources,

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:57.200
globally and
privately, which means

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.500
that you don't need to assign
public IPs to your VMs,

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.560
and you don't need to set
up expensive VPN or peering

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.380
connections in order to
stitch regional VPCs together.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.690
Now, another way of
designing for robustness

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:14.090
is to use load balancing.

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:16.460
Due to the global nature
of Google's network,

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.130
we are able to offer global
load balancing so that you'll

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.740
get a global IP address
assigned to your load-balanced

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.390
application.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.000
So a user in Taiwan sees
the same global IP address

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.280
for your application
as a user in Texas,

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:32.540
and they both get routed to
the closest healthy backends.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.210
So the user in Texas
may get routing

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.470
to the closest healthy
backend that may

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:37.970
be running in Portland, Oregon.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.350
But if there are no
healthy backends that

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.170
are running in any
region in North America,

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:48.380
they may get routed to the next
closest healthy backend, which

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.300
may be across the Atlantic.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.770
But the important thing here is
that the end user's TCP or SSL

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.140
connection will get
terminated close

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.320
to the location of the user
before getting encrypted

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.800
and then routed over the
Google network backbone

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.030
to the data center in Europe.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:09.420
We use the same DDoS
protection system in Cloud

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.820
as we do for the rest of
Google, and so you benefit

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.580
from the same perimeter
security that the rest of Google

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.370
services have.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.180
We are able to assign
a single global IP

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:24.750
address to our load-balanced
applications using Stabilized

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.140
Anycast.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.380
Now, Anycast, as many
of you would know,

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.480
is an addressing and
routing methodology

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.710
that allows you to wrote data
grants from a single sender

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:38.430
to the receiver that is closest
to the sender among a set

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:41.970
of multiple receivers that are
all programmed to self-traffic

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:47.400
and to receive traffic
on the same IP address.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.410
We use Stabilized
Anycast in order

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.610
to preserve the BGP session,
despite BGP instability.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:58.500
So if an end user's ISP is
recalculating the routes

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.080
that they are
announcing, then there

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.770
may be a certain amount of
instability in the TCP sessions

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.200
that the end user experiences.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.180
As a result of this,
the end user's request

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.090
may get reported to a
network load balancer

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.900
in a location that is not
closest to the end user.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.030
Now when this happens,
the network load balancer

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:21.150
in that location figures out
that the client IP is actually

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.710
closer to a different location.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:27.450
And then forwards that
request to that location.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.860
So this is how we basically
preserve TCP session stability

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:33.212
despite BGP instability.

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:38.870
So that's all about
the physical network.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:41.370
How does this enable features
in the global network,

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.159
in the virtual network?

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:46.270
So as you know, virtual networks
services in Google Cloud

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:49.160
are global in nature.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.310
So you can create
your network once,

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:55.210
you can associate network
policies once in your network.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:57.520
So things like firewall
rules and routing policies

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.130
can be applied once
to your network,

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:02.110
and these policies
will work seamlessly

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:04.420
as you expand to
multiple regions

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:06.970
and you place your compute
resources in new regions.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.260
If you're an enterprise
network administrator,

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:14.540
then you can use
Shared VPC in order

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:17.360
to centrally create and
manage your network,

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.330
while allowing full
autonomy to your developers

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:22.370
and allowing your
organization to scale

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:25.650
to hundreds or even thousands
of developers or development

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:26.150
teams.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:30.910
So let's take a
look at an example.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:32.410
Now let's say, in
your organization,

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:34.720
you would like to create
two types of applications.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.090
One is a web application
that is internet-facing,

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.190
and the other is a
billing application that

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.690
is not facing the internet.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:46.150
With Shared VPC, you can
create a load balancer

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:48.580
in your web app project.

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:50.460
For reasons that we
discussed earlier,

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.120
you'll want to
create redundancy,

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.910
and so you create compute
resources in multiple regions.

00:09:55.910 --> 00:10:00.040
And then you can set these as
backends of your load balancer.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:02.920
You can then create internal
load-balanced applications

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:05.020
inside the billing
project, and you

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:10.450
can use your billing VMs as
backends to these internal load

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:12.010
balancers.

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:15.070
And thus you can create
multiple tiers of applications

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:16.675
in your share VPC.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.770
You can then associate
firewall rules within the VPC

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:26.070
in order to restrict traffic.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:27.990
Due to the global
nature of our network,

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:31.110
you also get access to
Google-managed API services

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.320
privately, which means that you
don't need to assign public IP

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.890
addresses to your VMs
in order to access API

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:41.580
services like your Cloud
Storage, Machine Learning, Big

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.910
Tables, Banner, and many others.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:48.270
This functionality of privately
accessing Google-managed API

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:51.330
services is now
extended to the on-prem

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:54.090
so that you are able to
access Google-managed API

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:57.420
services privately from
your on-prem network via VPN

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:58.487
or interconnect.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.320
In order to secure your VPC, you
can use network layer firewall

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:05.730
rules.

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:08.340
Network layer firewall
rules are stateful and

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.520
connection-tracked.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.950
Now, you can create, allow,
or deny firewall rules,

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.960
either in the ingress
or the egress direction,

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:20.860
by specifying source IP ranges
or destination IP ranges.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:22.980
You can also use tags
or service accounts

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:25.500
in order to easily
group resources that you

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:28.720
are applying firewall rules on.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:30.980
Now, another really
important feature

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:33.240
is enable and disable
firewall rules.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.502
This is something that we have
launched in the past year.

00:11:36.502 --> 00:11:38.210
So if you're
troubleshooting your network

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:40.850
and you would like to find
out the root cause of an issue

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:43.520
by temporarily disabling
a firewall rule

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:47.870
and seeing what effect
it has on the network,

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:51.660
now you don't have to delete the
firewall rule and recreate it.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:56.833
You can simply disable and then
re-enable the firewall rule.

00:11:56.833 --> 00:11:58.500
Another really important
feature that we

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:02.700
have launched to GA over the
past year is firewall logging.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.410
Firewall logging
basically allows

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.870
you to see reports
of connection records

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:10.560
that get created for every time
a firewall tool gets applied

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.410
on a connection.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.657
Firewall logs are not
sampled, unlike VPC Flow Logs,

00:12:16.657 --> 00:12:18.990
but there is a limit on the
number of connection records

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:22.050
that get exported in a
five-second interval,

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:25.761
and this limit depends
on your machine size.

00:12:25.761 --> 00:12:29.760
The firewall logs get exported
to the shared VPC host project

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:31.290
so that the security
administrator

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:35.100
of your organization can
view the firewall rules

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:38.820
and verify that the firewall
rules in the organization

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:43.780
are administered and are
getting applied correctly.

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:46.320
You can export firewall
logs with Stackdriver,

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:50.550
Cloud Pub/Sub, Cloud
Storage, or BigQuery.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:53.527
Now, as I mentioned,
firewall logs

00:12:53.527 --> 00:12:55.860
consist of connection records,
where a connection record

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:59.310
gets created every time a
firewall rule gets applied.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:03.090
So for VM-to-VM traffic, there
would be a connection record

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:07.050
that gets created for the
egress rule on the sender VM,

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:09.570
and for the ingress rule that
gets applied on the receiver

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.670
VM.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:16.500
This is also true for
VM-to-VM traffic in the VPC,

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:20.760
even if the VMs belong to
multiple service projects.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:23.850
And for traffic that is
entering or leaving your VPC,

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:29.070
either to go to a peered
VPC, to go to the internet,

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:32.248
or to go to your on-prem
through a VPN connection.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.860
Now, if you would like to
apply security policies

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.750
at the edge of our
network, then you

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:45.130
can do so by creating security
policies using Cloud Armor.

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:46.720
And what this
allows you to do is

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:50.480
to specify the rules that
should get applied at the edge.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.000
So this is perimeter
security that

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:55.160
gets applied on your
load-balanced applications.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.000
So let's say you create a
load-balanced application.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.480
You can now associate security
policies, using Cloud Armor,

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.610
with the load balancer.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:05.200
And this ensures
that the traffic that

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:08.740
is permitted to the
load-balanced application

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:12.280
will conform with the rules
that you have specified.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:17.590
So your VMs basically don't
see traffic from the senders

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:20.560
that you have blacklisted.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:23.800
You can specify Cloud Armor
rules using IP blacklists

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:26.680
or whitelists.

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:28.460
This set of rules,
this functionality

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:31.000
is basically generally
available now.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:34.660
You can also specify the
rules using [INAUDIBLE] rules

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:38.425
or geospecific rules, or you can
use a flexible rules language

00:14:38.425 --> 00:14:41.050
in order to customize the rules
that you would like to specify.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.120
So what does a typical
deployment look like?

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:49.130
Now, in a typical deployment,
you would create an HTTP

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:51.680
or HTTPS load balancer-- and
this is a global load-balanced

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.030
application--

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:56.540
And You would automatically get
defense against DDoS attacks.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:01.887
This is because, at the edge,
we implement DDoS protection,

00:15:01.887 --> 00:15:03.970
and so any traffic that
is entering on our network

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:07.120
will get DDoS attacks
defense for free.

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:11.860
If you would like to
apply custom rules

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:13.528
for your load-balanced
application,

00:15:13.528 --> 00:15:15.070
then you can use
Cloud Armor, and you

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:17.380
can associate the
Cloud Armor security

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:20.890
policy with your load balancer.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:22.930
If you would like
to allow access

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:24.520
to your load-balanced
application

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:28.360
only for users that have been
granted this access using

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:33.220
IM policies, then you can use
a product called Cloud IAP.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:36.550
That's short for
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:40.390
So what the identity-aware proxy
does is that it checks whether

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:43.450
the end user's
credentials have--

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:46.630
it will check the end user's
credentials against IAM policy,

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.110
and it will check whether
the end user has been granted

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:53.080
IAM access to this
load-balancing application,

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:57.650
and if it has, then it will
allow the traffic to come in.

00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:59.890
So traffic will
basically enter your VPC.

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:03.190
It will be received
by the VMs only

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:05.680
if it is allowed both
by Cloud Armor as well

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:07.180
as the identity-aware proxy.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.190
Within the VPC, you can
use network layer firewall

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:16.130
rules to specify the
security on your VMs,

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:18.850
and you can specify them so
that are allowing traffic only

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:21.220
from the load balancer
proxy, and you

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:25.030
don't have to open your VMs
or your ports on your VMs

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:25.720
to the internet.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:33.190
Another really important
aspect of security design

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:37.810
is ensuring that you mitigate
the risk of data exfiltration.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:41.020
You can do this now using
VPC service controls

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:44.290
so that you define a service
perimeter or a security

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:48.040
perimeter outside of which your
data should not be accessible

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:53.210
or it should not be
allowed to be copied.

00:16:53.210 --> 00:16:58.040
With the functionality of
allowing private access

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.140
to Google services from
your on-prem network

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:03.740
by a VPN or interconnects,
the definition

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:05.333
of your security
perimeter can also

00:17:05.333 --> 00:17:06.500
be extended to your on-prem.

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:12.750
So that brings us to
the next question--

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:14.900
how do you access on-prem?

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:17.960
Now, there are multiple ways of
connecting your virtual network

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:20.930
running in the cloud to
your on-prem network.

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:24.349
You can do so using
VPN, and you can either

00:17:24.349 --> 00:17:26.390
configure a VPN
using static routes

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:28.550
or you can use Cloud
Router in order

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:32.240
to dynamically exchange
rounds using BGP.

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:34.130
Or you can use
interconnect, in which case

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:35.510
you're directly peering with us.

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:37.520
And you can use either
dedicated interconnect,

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:39.350
where you control
the peering, or you

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:40.990
can use partner
interconnect, where

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:43.242
you are paying a partner for--

00:17:43.242 --> 00:17:45.200
where the partner is
basically peering with us,

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.450
and you're paying them for
the bandwidth that you use.

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:53.630
You can create VPN connections
or interconnect attachments

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:57.070
in the shared VPC host project,
and the virtual machines

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:59.570
and all the service projects
that are attached to the shared

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:04.580
network will then get access
to your on-prem via the VPN

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:06.471
connection or the interconnect.

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:10.620
We're really
excited to announce,

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:13.050
now, the option to create
highly-available VPN

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:14.540
connections.

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:18.000
With highly-available VPN,
you can create two interfaces

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.820
on your VPN gateway,
and you can connect

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:23.820
the peer gateway on your
on-prem to these two

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:25.080
different interfaces.

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:28.020
Each of these interfaces
gets a different IP address,

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:30.210
and this allows redundancy
in your connection

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:33.470
to your on-prem network.

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:35.690
You can either use
highly-available VPN

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:37.870
in active-active
mode, in which case

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:41.990
you're advertising the routes
from your on-prem using

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:44.440
the same [INAUDIBLE] value
or the same priority,

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:46.460
and you would need
to use the same base

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:48.740
priority as well
when advertising

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:51.430
those routes to your VPC.

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:54.920
And in this case both
the tunnels will be used,

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:56.750
and the traffic
will be easy, and be

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:59.600
hashed over both the tunnels.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:02.630
Or you can use it an
active-passive mode, where

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:05.600
one of the tunnels is
used while it is up,

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.280
and when we figure out that
the connectivity is down,

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:11.840
then we fall back
to the other tunnel.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.000
This basically increases
the availability

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.510
of your VPN connection,
your on-prem, by 90%.

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:21.335
And so in a single region, you
get four-nines availability

00:19:21.335 --> 00:19:22.960
for connecting to
your on-prem network.

00:19:26.102 --> 00:19:27.810
Now, the next thing
that we have launched

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:30.180
to [INAUDIBLE] over the
past year is Cloud NAT.

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:33.030
Now, Cloud NAT is a feature
that a lot of our enterprise

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:35.580
customers have been asking
for, and they're really

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:38.940
excited to use.

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:41.520
Cloud NAT is basically
a managed NAT solution

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:43.320
that allows you to
configure access

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.930
to the internet on
your virtual machines

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:49.580
without having to give your
virtual machines public IPs.

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:53.590
We implement outbound NAT, we
don't implement inbound NAT.

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:56.850
And so this increases
the security of your VPC

00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:00.210
by ensuring that connections
cannot be initiated from

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:03.210
a malicious user on the internet
to your virtual machines

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:06.020
running in the Cloud.

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:08.430
Cloud NAT basically
scales seamlessly

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:10.710
across VMs and
across connections

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:15.004
by handling both static as
well as dynamic IP allocation.

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:21.997
You can configure NAT as well
in the shared VPC host project

00:20:21.997 --> 00:20:24.330
in the regions where you have
virtual machines that need

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:27.010
connectivity to the internet.

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:29.820
So let's imagine that you have
a package server that you would

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:33.450
like to download packages from,
onto your virtual machines

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:38.750
running in your VPC, before
you can bootstrap them.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:42.770
Now, after you've configured the
NAT gateways in your shared VPC

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:45.620
host project, the VMs in
all the service projects

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:48.710
that are attached
to the shared VPC

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:50.500
will be able to
access the packet

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:55.480
server using the IPs that are
managed by the NAT gateway.

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.700
One thing that is really
important to mention here

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:01.060
is that NAT is a
control plane component,

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:03.400
it's not a proxy-based solution.

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:05.980
And so you get the same
bandwidth and performance

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:08.590
for your internet
connection as you

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:13.150
would if you were to give
public IPs to your VMs.

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:15.760
So NAT basically
scales really well.

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:19.030
You need a single NAT
gateway, because it's not

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:21.910
a proxy-based solution,
in order to manage

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:26.650
the NAT IPs for thousands of
VMs in that region in your VPC.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:34.510
Now, if you assign public IP
addresses to your VMs primarily

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:37.360
for getting connectivity
to the internet,

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:40.930
but you also had the added
advantage of being able to SSH

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:43.210
to your VMs using
those public IPs,

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:46.240
then you lose that capability
when you remove public IPs

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:49.770
and when you switch
to Cloud NAT,

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:53.580
because connections cannot be
initiated from outside the VPC

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:56.400
using Cloud NAT.

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:59.115
With Cloud IAP-- and
Cloud IAP is something

00:21:59.115 --> 00:22:00.810
we discussed briefly
in the context

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:03.820
of global load-balanced
applications.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:07.230
Cloud IAP is now extended
to have functionality

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:08.910
for TCP forwarding.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:10.680
And what this
allows you to do is

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:15.780
to specify IM policies on who
has access to SSH to your VMs.

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:19.500
And when a request
comes in, the proxy

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:21.120
will basically check
whether the user

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:26.490
has been granted permission
to SSH access to the VM.

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:29.820
And then if that check passes,
then the SSH connection

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:35.190
will be wrapped inside HTTPS
and then, using TCP forwarding,

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:37.560
will be sent to the remote
instance running in your VPC.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:43.760
Now, coming to the
next topic, how do you

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:47.240
access manage services?

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:51.150
Managed services can be
accessed by using VPC peering.

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:54.770
So if you want to get full-mesh
connectivity between two VPCs

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:57.230
that are running in cloud--
they may be in different

00:22:57.230 --> 00:23:00.000
organizations or the
same organization--

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.750
then you can use VPC peering.

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:05.660
We're really excited to announce
the general availability

00:23:05.660 --> 00:23:07.540
of private service access.

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:10.490
Now, private service access
is a managed solution

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:12.890
that allows you to get a
private connection to a managed

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.050
service.

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:16.970
And the other really
cool thing that it does

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:20.060
is it allows you, as
a service consumer,

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:23.810
to specify a global IP range
and hand this off to the service

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:26.900
provider so that all the
sub-networks in the service

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:31.590
provider's VPC get carved
out of this global IP range.

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:34.650
And so, as a consumer, you're
able to better plan for the IP

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:36.720
ranges that are used for
your managed services.

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:41.110
One of the really
important features

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:45.670
that we have added as
functionality to VPC peering

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:50.860
is the ability to access peer
VPCs from your on-prem network.

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:55.750
And so if you have a VPC, and
you are accessing a managed

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:59.470
service through VPC peering,
and you have a VPN connection

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:01.650
from your on-prem to
your VPC, then you

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:03.360
are able to also
access the managed

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:06.940
services from the on-prem,
via VPN or interconnect,

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:08.140
to your VPC.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:13.710
The other feature
that we have launched,

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:16.190
which is now available
in beta, is the ability

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:19.370
to control custom
route exchanges.

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:23.340
So let's take a look at all
of these in more detail.

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.520
So let's say you
have two networks.

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:27.380
The one on the left
is a consumer VPC

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:30.230
and the one on the
right is a producer VPC.

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:33.050
These two are in
different organizations.

00:24:33.050 --> 00:24:35.620
And once you pair
these two VPCs,

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:37.640
you get full-mesh
connectivity between all

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:42.170
the virtual machines
in both these VPCs.

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:44.510
And so you're able to
access the managed service

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:47.620
VMs from virtual machines
in different regions

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:52.640
in your consumer VPC, as well as
in different service projects.

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:55.220
So basically, you get
full-mesh connectivity.

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:58.160
Now let's imagine an
example where the consumer

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:02.880
VPC basically has added custom
routes in their routing table.

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:05.390
So on the consumer
VPC's routing table,

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:09.050
you can see the default local
routes for the subnets that

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:12.530
are created in the consumer
VPC, you can see the peer route

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:16.100
for the subnet that is
created in the peer VPC,

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.860
and you see two
additional static routes.

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:22.645
So one of them is a static
road to the VPN tunnel.

00:25:22.645 --> 00:25:24.020
So that's basically
a route going

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:27.090
to the consumer's on-prem.

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:30.030
And there's another route
that is configured to next hop

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:31.380
to a VM.

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:33.060
So let's say you have
an appliance that

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:35.790
is running on the VM.

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:40.080
On the peer side, you're able
to see the default local route

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:43.890
to the subnet on the
peer VPC, and you're also

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:47.160
able to see the peer
routes to the subnets that

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:49.830
are added in the consumer VPC.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:53.910
However, by default, you cannot
see the custom routes that get

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:56.190
added in the consumer VPC.

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:59.840
And so the route to the VPN
as well as the route to the VM

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:03.310
appliance are not visible in the
routing table of the producer

00:26:03.310 --> 00:26:07.090
VPC, by default.

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:10.850
With the ability to exchange
custom routes over VPC peering,

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:15.890
these routes will now be
visible in the producer VPC.

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:18.320
And so if you enable
export in the consumer

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:22.105
and you enable import of
custom routes on the producer,

00:26:22.105 --> 00:26:23.480
the producer's
routing table will

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:25.850
be populated with the
static routes that are

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:27.740
defined on the consumer VPC.

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:33.440
So here you can see the 10.4/16
goes to the consumer's on-prem

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:37.250
via VPN, as well as the
static route, 10.5/16,

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:41.120
which goes to the VM appliance,
are now visible in the routing

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:42.380
table of the producer VPC.

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:48.410
You can disable this by either
disabling export of custom

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:53.570
routes or by disabling import of
custom routes on the receiving

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:55.310
VPC.

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:57.830
Now, both export of
custom routes as well as

00:26:57.830 --> 00:27:00.930
import of custom routes
are disabled by default.

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:02.840
And so in order to
exchange custom routes,

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:07.460
you need to enable
export on the sender VPC

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:09.740
and enable import
on the receiver VPC.

00:27:14.032 --> 00:27:15.740
Now, the next topic
I'll be talking about

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:17.942
is that of VPC flow logs.

00:27:17.942 --> 00:27:21.460
Now, VPC flow logs are
basically a feature

00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:24.700
that we announced and
launched last year,

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:27.160
and we have enhanced
this product in order

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:31.570
to allow you to have more
control over the flow log size

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:34.150
that gets generated.

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:37.420
The default aggregation
interval for VPC flow logs

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:38.800
is five seconds.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:40.630
But now you can
configure this and change

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:45.740
it to be anywhere between five
seconds to up to 15 minutes.

00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:48.130
You can also configure
the flow report sampling.

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:51.420
By default, the sampling
is 50%, and you can now

00:27:51.420 --> 00:27:55.533
configure it to be anywhere
between 0% and 100%.

00:27:55.533 --> 00:27:57.700
So this basically allows
you to control the flow log

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:00.490
size that gets generated.

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:03.730
We also add certain
metadata to flow logs,

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:08.670
and there is no an option
to exclude this metadata.

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:10.350
And with that, I
would like to invite

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:13.350
Marshall onstage to share with
us a few recent announcements

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:14.430
on Cloud DNS.

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:21.250
MARSHALL VALE: Thank you, Neha.

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:22.610
So my name is Marshall Vale.

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:26.270
I'm the product manager for
Cloud DNS here at Google Cloud.

00:28:26.270 --> 00:28:30.260
And so one of the key elements
of connecting your resources

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:32.700
together in your
VPC is, of course,

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:35.168
DNS, or the Domain Name System.

00:28:35.168 --> 00:28:36.710
Today I'm going to
give you a summary

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:39.770
of those types of capabilities
that Cloud DNS provides

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:41.360
for your VPC,
along with a couple

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:43.640
of exciting new announcements.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:46.940
Of course it all starts with
Cloud DNS private zones.

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:50.030
Private zones allows
internal DNS resolution

00:28:50.030 --> 00:28:51.810
to your private network.

00:28:51.810 --> 00:28:54.235
Now, it's important to keep
your internal resolution

00:28:54.235 --> 00:28:56.360
to your private networks
because that helps exclude

00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:59.000
external parties from
discovering information

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:00.500
about their
resources, of course,

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:02.330
on your private networks.

00:29:02.330 --> 00:29:05.540
Private zones can be
attached to a single network

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:07.886
or multiple networks
in your VPC.

00:29:07.886 --> 00:29:10.610
Private zones also
supports what's

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:13.460
called split horizon, which
allows you to do overlapping

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:15.480
public and private zones.

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:17.060
So for example, you
may have a portal

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:18.990
that looks different
for your employees

00:29:18.990 --> 00:29:22.170
than it looks different
from your customers.

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:24.940
Private zones also
supports IM policies.

00:29:24.940 --> 00:29:28.410
So you can delegate
administration capabilities,

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:31.590
edit, or view capabilities
for your zones.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:34.140
Pleased to announce that
private zones is now

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:37.620
in general availability.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:39.240
Also very excited
to announce, here

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:42.240
at Next, the availability
of Stackdriver logging

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:44.680
and monitoring for
your private zones.

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:48.780
This allows query logs
and counts about responses

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:51.030
to be logged to Stackdriver.

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:53.790
From there, you can store
it long term in Stackdriver,

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:55.650
but you can also use
Stackdriver's Pub/Sub

00:29:55.650 --> 00:29:58.440
capabilities to send that
long to other storage

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:01.560
locations, such as
BigQuery, but also on-prem

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:06.000
for your own storage
and analysis tools.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:09.220
The query logs that are
recorded are probably

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:12.210
very similar to bind logs
that you're familiar with.

00:30:12.210 --> 00:30:16.230
It stores information such
as Q name or R data, but also

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:21.895
specific GCP Cloud DNS things,
such as project ID or DNS

00:30:21.895 --> 00:30:22.395
policies.

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:28.970
So for the metrics
and the monitoring,

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:31.420
that records information
such as serve

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:34.880
fails or annexed domain counts.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:36.860
And this is all really
important because it

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:41.580
helps you debug your DNS
situations on your VPC.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:44.870
But also it's important
for security analysis,

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:47.145
for threats in your system.

00:30:47.145 --> 00:30:48.770
Pleased to announce
that, here at Next,

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:50.810
it's now available
in public beta.

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:56.840
So I've been talking about Cloud
DNS services within your VPC,

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.240
but you also may need to
connect your DNS services

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:01.170
from your VPC to
other locations,

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:04.370
such as on-prem or
even another VPC.

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:07.190
So for connecting to
on-prem, we have a capability

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:09.080
called DNS forwarding.

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:11.540
This allows bi-directional
DNS resolution

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:15.380
from your GCP resources
to your on-prem resources.

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:19.880
Now, DNS outbound forwarding
allows your GCP resources

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:24.320
to resolve your hostnames
using an on-prem authoritative

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:27.260
server, such as Bind
or Active Directory.

00:31:27.260 --> 00:31:31.060
DNS inbound forwarding
does the opposite.

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:33.470
It allows your on-prem
sources to use Cloud DNS

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:35.510
as your authoritative server.

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:37.970
You can learn a little bit
more about DNS forwarding,

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:41.060
and of course your variety
of hybrid connection options

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:44.150
in the Net 2.04 session.

00:31:44.150 --> 00:31:48.120
DNS forwarding is
currently in public beta.

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:50.750
I'm also very
excited to announce

00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:52.940
availability of DNS peering.

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:55.430
This allows you to
cross DNS resolution

00:31:55.430 --> 00:31:57.385
across multiple VPCs.

00:31:57.385 --> 00:31:58.760
Let's look at a
couple scenarios.

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:01.250
First is, say you
are a SaaS provider

00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:04.400
and you want to connect with
the VPC and your consumer.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:07.550
The consumer would be
creating a special DNS peering

00:32:07.550 --> 00:32:11.330
zone that would connect to
the network and the service

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:15.350
providers in PPC, and they
would have their own zone

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:18.380
that the final resolution
would happen from.

00:32:18.380 --> 00:32:21.022
Or you might want to combine
this with DNS forwarding

00:32:21.022 --> 00:32:22.480
to create an
architecture where you

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:25.880
have multiple VPCs that can
use an on-prem authoritative

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:26.540
resolver.

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:28.670
From there, you would
make a single VPC that

00:32:28.670 --> 00:32:31.210
would be a hub VPC
doing DNS forwarding,

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:33.800
and you have multiple
spoke VPCs that

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:38.780
would use DNS peering to
connect into the hub VPC.

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:42.560
DNS peering even supports
resolution for your .internal

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:44.720
addresses.

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:49.400
Here at Next, DNS peering is
now available in public beta.

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:53.270
So with that, you can see
a summary of the Cloud DNS

00:32:53.270 --> 00:32:56.180
services for your private
zones, and the wide variety

00:32:56.180 --> 00:33:00.370
of flexibility that it supports
in your VPC architectures.

00:33:00.370 --> 00:33:02.260
So with that, I'll
pass it back to Neha

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:04.302
to give you a summary of
what you've heard today.

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:11.330
NEHA PATTAN: Thanks, Marshall.

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:15.170
So to summarize
three main takeaways

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:20.420
that we would like you to
focus on from our presentation

00:33:20.420 --> 00:33:23.400
is that VPCs are
global in nature,

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:26.043
they're built on top of a
global network backbone,

00:33:26.043 --> 00:33:28.460
and you're assured that the
traffic basically never leaves

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:31.550
the network backbone.

00:33:31.550 --> 00:33:35.120
You get access to all of you
compute and storage resources,

00:33:35.120 --> 00:33:37.580
globally and
privately, so you don't

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:40.460
have to assign public IPs
to your virtual machines

00:33:40.460 --> 00:33:43.610
in order to access
managed Google

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:47.370
services like Cloud storage.

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:49.680
You're able to configure
your global network once.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:52.050
You are able to associate
network policies

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:54.240
with this network,
and you're also

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:56.100
able to centrally
create and manage

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:58.380
the network in your
organization while allowing

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:00.870
your organization
to scale to hundreds

00:34:00.870 --> 00:34:04.570
or even thousands of developers
or development teams.

00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:08.500
Security comes first
with Cloud VPC.

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:10.840
You can apply network
layer firewall rules

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:13.690
for specifying security
within your VPC.

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:15.610
These firewall rules
are stateful and

00:34:15.610 --> 00:34:17.730
connection-tracked.

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:21.760
You can specify Cloud
Armor security policies.

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:25.197
In order to apply
rules that get enforced

00:34:25.197 --> 00:34:27.239
at the edge of our network
for your load-balanced

00:34:27.239 --> 00:34:31.620
applications, and you can
also use VPC service controls

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:34.574
in order to mitigate the
risk of data exfiltration.

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:39.670
VPC features are
basically integrated

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.800
with Stackdriver logging.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:45.250
And now, with VPC flow
logs and firewall logging,

00:34:45.250 --> 00:34:50.070
it is really easy
to monitor VPCs.

00:34:50.070 --> 00:34:52.929
With that, I would like
to invite Ed on stage

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:56.889
to share with us Cardinal
Health's story on how

00:34:56.889 --> 00:34:58.520
they deploy and use VPCs.

00:34:58.520 --> 00:34:59.020
Thanks.

00:35:03.715 --> 00:35:05.090
JONATHAN EDWARD
HAMMOND: I should

00:35:05.090 --> 00:35:08.210
start by saying this is
a really great session.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.770
I've actually watched
it a couple times.

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:13.180
So for those on
video, you might want

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:15.830
to go over it a couple times.

00:35:15.830 --> 00:35:20.060
In Cardinal Health, we're
a Fortune 500 company,

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:22.430
and we have used a
lot of the features

00:35:22.430 --> 00:35:24.380
that you've heard today.

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:26.930
We're a global
enterprise, and so we

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:28.910
have services running all over.

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:31.460
Primarily, our first
deployments in GCP

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:33.440
had been within
the United States,

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:37.490
but we have plans and
designs to fully go global,

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:41.400
and we've provisioned a lot
of that in our networks.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:45.540
Our business objectives
in adopting cloud

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:49.380
is to largely be
quick to market,

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:54.300
be able to adopt different
technologies very quickly,

00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:57.210
and be very cost-effective.

00:35:57.210 --> 00:35:59.590
Obviously, in all
our businesses,

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:02.790
we want to try and save money,
and so were always interested

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:05.250
in trying to be the most
effective with every dollar

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:06.713
we spend.

00:36:06.713 --> 00:36:08.130
The other thing
that we have to do

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:10.172
is we have to make sure
that we're protecting all

00:36:10.172 --> 00:36:12.193
our health care information.

00:36:12.193 --> 00:36:14.610
There's lots of regulations
in various different countries

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:17.580
about health care information,
so protecting our customer

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:20.550
information is very key to us.

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:24.450
As far as having
agility, and being

00:36:24.450 --> 00:36:27.210
able to be flexible,
and speed to market,

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:31.650
we want to try and move
our shared services'

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:34.770
traditional structure
more into a DevOps

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:38.040
model, where
development teams can

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:40.230
provision their own services.

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:43.930
But we still need to
make sure it's secure.

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:47.850
So we need to provide
techniques and capabilities

00:36:47.850 --> 00:36:50.580
where they can be
very agile, very

00:36:50.580 --> 00:36:52.740
flexible, without
having to say mother

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:56.168
may I, if you will,
but still keep

00:36:56.168 --> 00:36:58.710
all those guardrails in place
so that they don't do something

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:01.050
that would be compromising
to our customer data

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:03.030
or to the enterprise.

00:37:03.030 --> 00:37:05.490
And of course I mentioned
the cost-effectiveness.

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:08.320
Obviously that's a key aspect.

00:37:08.320 --> 00:37:10.920
So what we chose
to do early on was

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:12.940
we created two host projects.

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:15.510
So we have two host
projects that basically

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:17.940
do all the network things.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:20.880
So that what that does is,
for our application teams,

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:23.580
they don't have to think about
all those network pieces.

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:25.580
All the interconnects and
all that kind of stuff

00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:27.790
is hidden from them.

00:37:27.790 --> 00:37:29.730
And then we have hundreds
of service projects

00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:33.000
that sit on top of that,
and they use these two host

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:34.740
projects.

00:37:34.740 --> 00:37:37.410
We have one for production,
one for non-production.

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:40.980
Each one of those
have multiple VPCs.

00:37:40.980 --> 00:37:45.810
And those service projects are
then allocated or authorized

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:48.780
to be used by various
different development teams.

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:53.550
Those development teams then
are able to utilize the network.

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:56.413
One thing that-- if you're
in the network world

00:37:56.413 --> 00:37:58.080
and you've talked to
a lot of developers

00:37:58.080 --> 00:37:59.880
for a length of
time, you'll find

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:03.810
that you start talking about
routes, and BGP, and firewalls,

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:05.343
and their eyes
glaze over and they

00:38:05.343 --> 00:38:06.510
don't want to hear about it.

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:09.210
I don't know about you, but
I've run into a lot of that.

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:12.280
So we try and make it very,
very easy for our consumers,

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:15.330
our consumers being the
development staff that are

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:17.760
actually helping the business.

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:18.670
And in that regard.

00:38:18.670 --> 00:38:20.230
There's lots of
need for training,

00:38:20.230 --> 00:38:22.110
there's lots of need
for documentation,

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:24.570
and that is a continual process.

00:38:24.570 --> 00:38:26.760
And our company, it's
very large, and we

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:29.050
have lots of people coming
and going all the time,

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:31.950
so we're constantly doing this
education over and over again--

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:36.840
documentation, training
videos, in-house webcasts,

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:38.700
and that kind of
thing that we do

00:38:38.700 --> 00:38:41.123
are very, very key to
our strategy there.

00:38:41.123 --> 00:38:43.290
We also have to make sure
we have all the governance

00:38:43.290 --> 00:38:47.730
controls in place,
both proactive

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:50.220
and reactive
governance controls.

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:53.220
That's really critical to
success in a highly-regulated

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:57.270
industry such as [? we. ?] So
the value of the host projects,

00:38:57.270 --> 00:39:01.140
again, is that I have a small
number of network experts--

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:03.120
very small number
of experts that

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:06.310
know how all this
networking thing works.

00:39:06.310 --> 00:39:08.970
So all the interconnects,
VPN tunnels,

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:13.140
VPC peering, how all
that gets put together,

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:15.780
all that physics that's
behind the scenes,

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:17.805
we try and make it simple
and easy to consume.

00:39:20.560 --> 00:39:23.040
We have a few centralized
appliances that we use.

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:25.380
I gave a presentation
earlier this week

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:27.720
about how we used some
centralized appliances

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:30.450
to do some of the more
traditional security perimeter

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:34.170
work that you might expect
in a large enterprise that's

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:35.770
been around for a
long period of time.

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:37.260
And we're moving to the cloud.

00:39:37.260 --> 00:39:41.720
We have a lot of technologies
that we have to bring with us.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:44.870
We also have needed, at times,
to create a separate subnet.

00:39:44.870 --> 00:39:47.110
Say, for instance,
there's a service project

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:49.490
that they have a
very specific need.

00:39:49.490 --> 00:39:52.480
So we can create a
subnet just for them,

00:39:52.480 --> 00:39:56.600
and allow just that service
project access to that subnet.

00:39:56.600 --> 00:39:59.590
So therefore, nobody else knows
about it, just this one service

00:39:59.590 --> 00:40:00.190
project.

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:01.160
That's pretty powerful.

00:40:01.160 --> 00:40:03.940
We've used that a
couple of times.

00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:05.920
As far as firewall rule
management-- again,

00:40:05.920 --> 00:40:08.200
this is one of those
governance controls.

00:40:08.200 --> 00:40:11.170
We use firewall
logging extensively.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:14.440
That was a great feature that
we looked forward to, and have

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:18.450
been using it since the
day it was released--

00:40:18.450 --> 00:40:20.090
or available to us.

00:40:20.090 --> 00:40:23.050
We've been using it for a while.

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:24.920
But we use service accounts.

00:40:24.920 --> 00:40:27.655
So the goal here is that
I have a VM instance, say,

00:40:27.655 --> 00:40:31.660
and another VM instance, and
it's in my own service project.

00:40:31.660 --> 00:40:34.090
We allow the target and
the source to be the same,

00:40:34.090 --> 00:40:36.700
and we allow ingress and
egress rules for those

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:38.200
so they can talk to each other.

00:40:38.200 --> 00:40:43.310
So it's wide open any, so I
have a collection, if you will.

00:40:43.310 --> 00:40:45.700
Within my service
project, I can grant that.

00:40:45.700 --> 00:40:47.830
If I won't go between
two service projects,

00:40:47.830 --> 00:40:50.140
then I go service account
to service account.

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:52.900
What that does is it gives
us pretty good segmentation

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:57.430
across the different projects,
and pretty tight controls

00:40:57.430 --> 00:40:58.920
in that regard.

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:05.140
When we have a standard pattern,
like a north-south pattern,

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:09.850
for example, an
internal web server.

00:41:09.850 --> 00:41:14.020
An internal web server
is a standard 80 and 443,

00:41:14.020 --> 00:41:17.290
allowed in from the
RFC 19 address space.

00:41:17.290 --> 00:41:19.630
And so if that's
the case, then we

00:41:19.630 --> 00:41:21.340
allow them to put
on a network tag.

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:23.470
This has been
preapproved by security.

00:41:23.470 --> 00:41:26.680
And you apply that network
tag only to the VM instances

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:27.700
where that applies.

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:31.157
So not everybody gets it,
but now, as a DevOps team,

00:41:31.157 --> 00:41:32.740
I don't have to go
to the network team

00:41:32.740 --> 00:41:34.090
to do a firewall rule.

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:37.240
I just apply that tag, I
get those firewall rules.

00:41:37.240 --> 00:41:39.250
And in some cases
we have network tags

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:44.110
that apply both at ingress,
egress, and routing rules.

00:41:44.110 --> 00:41:46.180
For example, for
our egress, we use

00:41:46.180 --> 00:41:50.770
that to get out through
some security appliances

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:53.000
for some certain use cases.

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:57.350
So we do it based on routing
and firewall rules as well.

00:41:57.350 --> 00:41:59.890
And then we also
have cloud functions

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:02.640
that actually monitor some
of these firewall rules

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:05.060
so that we don't do
anything really dangerous.

00:42:05.060 --> 00:42:06.280
This is the reactive.

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:08.470
And so if somebody,
for some reason,

00:42:08.470 --> 00:42:13.110
creates a firewall rule that
was not supposed to be the case,

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:15.790
we shut that down
within seconds.

00:42:15.790 --> 00:42:17.320
I'm guilty of doing that.

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:18.650
I created a firewall rule.

00:42:18.650 --> 00:42:19.690
It was a 000.

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:22.030
I was doing it in a
very specific use case.

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:24.130
The function caught
me, I closed it down, I

00:42:24.130 --> 00:42:25.620
re-enabled it a couple times.

00:42:25.620 --> 00:42:27.970
I'm like, oh, I
know what's wrong,

00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:31.180
I didn't add myself
to the exclude list.

00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:32.270
So that was a good test.

00:42:32.270 --> 00:42:34.833
It actually worked, just
what we wanted it to do.

00:42:34.833 --> 00:42:36.250
The value of the
service projects,

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:38.410
again, is for the DevOps teams.

00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:42.010
So a DevOps team can actually
build all their resources.

00:42:42.010 --> 00:42:44.260
As you know, a
project is a bounce

00:42:44.260 --> 00:42:50.840
for authorization as well
as for costing, in our case.

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:53.290
We have costing
based on projects.

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:58.000
So with that, we can delegate
the accountability down

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:02.140
to a DevOps team, and let
them move on their own.

00:43:02.140 --> 00:43:04.630
Again, we advertise only--

00:43:04.630 --> 00:43:08.710
I say advertise, it's
grant the permission to--

00:43:08.710 --> 00:43:11.990
only selected subnets
to these projects.

00:43:11.990 --> 00:43:15.130
So rather than seeing all
the VPCs that we built run

00:43:15.130 --> 00:43:17.560
and seeing all the
subnets that we built,

00:43:17.560 --> 00:43:20.200
we only advertise
selected subnets.

00:43:20.200 --> 00:43:24.430
So say, for instance, your
project wants to run in Europe

00:43:24.430 --> 00:43:26.010
and only in Europe,
I'm not going

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:28.930
to show you any of the subnets
that are in the United States.

00:43:28.930 --> 00:43:31.900
Same way in the United
States, or in Asia, whatever.

00:43:31.900 --> 00:43:37.060
So that's very,
very powerful to us.

00:43:37.060 --> 00:43:38.468
So then the
developers don't have

00:43:38.468 --> 00:43:40.510
to start thinking about
all these network things.

00:43:40.510 --> 00:43:43.862
It's US Central 1 or US East 1.

00:43:43.862 --> 00:43:45.070
That's a pretty simple thing.

00:43:45.070 --> 00:43:47.920
Developers can deal with that.

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:49.780
We also have an
organizational policy

00:43:49.780 --> 00:43:53.050
that prohibits the
creating of any instances

00:43:53.050 --> 00:43:55.610
with an external IP address.

00:43:55.610 --> 00:43:59.720
Obviously the NAT capability
was a powerful feature

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:01.950
in that regard as well.

00:44:01.950 --> 00:44:06.660
OK so to sort of summarize,
showing on this picture,

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:08.640
we have multiple different VPCs.

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:13.310
Each one is global network X, Y,
and Z. We have different VPCs,

00:44:13.310 --> 00:44:16.610
and those are provisioned
in the host project.

00:44:16.610 --> 00:44:18.740
Within any service
project, I may

00:44:18.740 --> 00:44:21.680
expose only one or two subnets.

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:24.860
And in this case, service
project B and service project A

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:28.370
are communicating to each
other from different regions--

00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:29.810
they can be global--

00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:33.320
over the Google backbone,
and that all works.

00:44:33.320 --> 00:44:36.470
And the firewall rules
that you see on there,

00:44:36.470 --> 00:44:38.970
I have illustration
of three of them.

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:42.390
The first one is the
internal web server.

00:44:42.390 --> 00:44:45.660
So that's a network
tag-based rule.

00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:48.320
So if I put that
on my server, I can

00:44:48.320 --> 00:44:52.520
receive 80 and 443
traffic in-- my decision

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:55.220
as a devout DevOps person.

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:57.780
And then the middle one
is the "service account

00:44:57.780 --> 00:44:58.720
to service account."

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:00.470
So if you're in that
same service account,

00:45:00.470 --> 00:45:02.720
you have ingress/egress
to all your peers,

00:45:02.720 --> 00:45:05.600
and that's an
"any" kind of rule.

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:07.730
And then the last one
is "service project

00:45:07.730 --> 00:45:08.623
to service project."

00:45:08.623 --> 00:45:10.040
So we're using,
as much as we can,

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:12.200
service account to
service account.

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:17.380
And that gives us that
point-to-point communication.

00:45:17.380 --> 00:45:21.760
Well, that concludes my
portion of the presentation.

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:23.328
I'll turn it back over to Neha.

00:45:23.328 --> 00:45:26.744
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:26.744 --> 00:45:28.700
NEHA PATTAN: Thanks, Ed.

00:45:28.700 --> 00:45:31.460
So we might not have too
much time for questions,

00:45:31.460 --> 00:45:36.500
but we'll be available outside
this room for Q&amp;A. So thanks

00:45:36.500 --> 00:45:41.180
so much, everyone, for coming,
and see you again next year.

00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:44.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

